{"authored" [{"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b519fcd567150762be2acc0f209e3599855b11460e4d739b98ee1f3be3f4b4fa" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/state.json" "file_sha256" "c2716842d162a295307704730cb6710fd5a7785273109a8f91056d11126b8d5f" "basis" 1209 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14030999/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/