-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-2x\\,=\\,2x(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}-1)\\,=\\,-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{2}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{(1)^{2}+1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e6025a13f23aeb6d27243ec300cc6b7e5c4e84f091a46053ad4b987d66214240" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selectio