{"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{9}-9,$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=9{x}^{8}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9{x}^{8}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =9(-1)^{8}=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =9(1)^{8}=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/state.json" "file_sha256" "f6d3e672e3e2faa23020a1c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-113764" 