c778d21f37b4b5a2986bc19ae090d5b92eecc72dc7" "basis" 1441 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040404/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)=4{x}^{4}+4{x}^{3}+9,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum\n- $x=0$ is a local minimum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=16{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}=4{x}^{2}(4x+3).\n$$\n\nAnd now find the stationary points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}(4x+3)=0\\,⟹\\,x=0\\,\\text{or}\\,x=-\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=-\\frac{3}{4},\\,0.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,-\\frac{1}{2},1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =4(-1)^{2}(4(-1)+3)=-4<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}(4(-\\frac{1}{2})+3)=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =4(1)^{2}(4+3)=28>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{3}{4})$ | $-\\frac{3}{4}$ | $(-\\frac{3}{4},0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-\\frac{3}{4},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative does not change the sign. Therefore, $x=0$ is neither a local minimum nor a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37c3d2153133fab37a0142eeb772f899abce66be675ddf7b6e0e3eef7050a892"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-21468" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the function $f(x)=x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ defined for $x\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum\n- $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum\n- $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$   on $(0,2\\pi )$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ are stationary points. Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points  $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\pi ,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi =3>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) & =1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=1-\\sqrt{3}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ | $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ | $(\\frac{5\\pi }{3},2\\pi )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ |  | $\\pi $ |  | $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3},$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ is a local maximum.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37c3d2153133fab37a0142eeb772f899abce66be675ddf7b6e0e3eef7050a892"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22810" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)={e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}$ have a local maximum?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (2x){e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}=-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x{e}^{-{x}^{2}/2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-(-1){e}^{-(-1)^{2}/2}={e}^{-1/2}>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-(1){e}^{-(1)^{2}/2}=-{e}^{-1/2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37c3d2153133fab37a0142eeb772f899abce66be675ddf7b6e0e3eef7050a892"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-e"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "e"] ["math" "{e}^{-1}"] ["text" "$f(x)$ does not have any local maxima"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-22820" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the function $f(x)={x}^{9}-9,$ which of the following statements are true?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=9{x}^{8}.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9{x}^{8}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is the only stationary point of $f(x).$ Next, to determine whether this point is local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =9(-1)^{8}=9>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =9(1)^{8}=9>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ |  | $↗$ |\n\nThe first derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, we conclude that $x=0$ is neither a minimum nor a maximum.\n\nHence, the first derivative test shows that the function has no local extrema." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37c3d2153133fab37a0142eeb772f899abce66be675ddf7b6e0e3eef7050a892"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local maximum at $x=1$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=0$"] ["text" "There is a local minimum at $x=1$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-113764" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The function $f(x)$ has no local extrema"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)-{x}^{2}$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}-2x\\,=\\,2x(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}-1)\\,=\\,-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{′}(x)=0$  gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}+1} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $x=0$ is a stationary point. Next, to determine whether this point is a local maximum or minimum, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-1,1$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)^{3}}{(-1)^{2}+1}=\\frac{2}{2}=1>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{2(1)^{3}}{(1)^{2}+1}=-\\frac{2}{2}=-1<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $0$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-1$ |  | $1$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical point.\n\n- At $x=0,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=0$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has a local maximum at $x=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "37c3d2153133fab37a0142eeb772f899abce66be675ddf7b6e0e3eef7050a892"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"} {"question" "q-344401" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062765/state.json" "file_sha256" "adb38f2d0ce8eb86765c9399bcb64dde0c920eccabb9f6e6181e584067fda254"}] "usage" {"q-344401" [] "q-22820" [] "q-113764" [] "q-21468" [] "q-22810" []} "basis" 2015 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186103007 17592186103008 17592186103009 17592186103010 17592186103011 17592186307730 1759218630